    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d333">

                <pb facs="0377" n="343" sameAs="frus:frus1897/pg343"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Sherman to Mr. Powell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1897-10-23"><hi rendition="#italic">October 23,
                        1897</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 42.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    No. 43 of the 7th instant, inclosing copy of the proposed law requiring all
                    persons leaving Haitian ports to provide themselves with passports.</p>
                <p>In reply I have to state that the requirement of a passport or permit to quit a
                    country is common and is enforced at the present time by important States, such
                    as Russia, Turkey, and Spain, in the Spanish Antilles. The right to prescribe
                    such a formality can not well be disputed, but the amount of the fee (6 gourds)
                    may warrant friendly representations against so onerous a charge.</p>
                <p>Respectfully, yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Sherman.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
